**PASS — 43/100.** Packet written to `docs/content-analysis/entity-gaps/Alexandr-Wang.md`.

## The call

Alexandr Wang is a good subject sitting in a bad SERP position. Those are different things, and this command measures the second one.

**What's strong:** demand is genuinely rising across three separate 2026 cycles (LeCun's FT attack in January, CNBC's one-year retrospective in June, Muse Code shipping Aug 5). The source record is one of the deepest I've surveyed — nine long-form first-person interviews with transcripts, a House Armed Services Committee witness bio, and four-plus named third-party voices. And there's a clean, ownable contradiction: Wang built a $29B company by industrializing *other people's* judgment, and is now being told by LeCun he was never an expert himself. Scored 14/15 on sources, 9/10 on angle.

**What kills it:** the exact-name SERP is saturated. Personal Wikipedia page, Meta's own leadership page, Forbes, Fortune, The Week, Entrepreneur — all on page one. That's 6/25 on SERP weakness plus the automatic −20 Wikipedia penalty. 63 − 20 = 43.

The command's own model names this exact case as *not* an entity gap. The only genuine weakness is the fact-query tail (age, parents, girlfriend), which is dominated by content farms — and that tail is low-value, partly unanswerable, and partly unsafe.

## Two things worth keeping

1. **`Alexandr Wang MBTI` returns personality-database.com.** That's a query family 9takes routinely beats, and it never touches the Wikipedia/Forbes biography SERP. Not an entity gap by this definition, but a real low-cost lane — recorded in the packet so it isn't lost.
2. **Re-audit within 7 days if Meta ruptures.** If Wang exits or MSL restructures, Wikipedia lags a fast story by weeks and the window becomes genuinely contested.

## Landmines flagged for the pipeline

The gossip farms are propagating a stale "28" — he was born January 1997, so he's 29 now; the packet says write the birth month-year instead. No partner should ever be named: Kiernan Shipka and Lucy Guo both circulate with zero evidentiary basis (Guo is his co-founder — it looks like pure proximity invention). And LeCun's Llama 4 benchmark-fudging claim must stay attributed to him every time — he left to found a competitor and holds a prior architectural bias against LLMs.

No blog, queue, or DB row was touched. I didn't apply the `−10` ambiguity penalty for the Alexander Wang fashion-designer collision, and said so explicitly in the packet — it's already priced into the 2/5 entity-clarity score, so charging it twice would have manufactured a lower number.

